-- =============================================================================
-- A multi-clause DECIDE is compiled to CONSIDERs that read its inputs, and to
-- local definitions holding the later clauses. None of the names those use
-- can meet a name the drafter wrote, in either direction (review of
-- legalese/l4-ide#545). Each #EVAL's answer is in its comment.
--
-- Printed back as source (`l4 batch`, the REPL, and the corpus's own re-print
-- check), a group is its clauses as written, so this holds there too.
-- =============================================================================
DECLARE Colour IS ONE OF Red, Green, Blue
DECLARE Shape IS ONE OF
  Circle HAS colour IS A Colour
  Square

-- A pattern variable named like a later input. The second column still tests
-- the second input, not the circle's colour. An earlier build reported both
-- groups as ambiguous at 1:1.
GIVEN s IS A Shape
      c IS A Colour
GIVETH A STRING
DECIDE paint (Circle c) Red IS "circle, paint red"
DECIDE paint s          c   IS "anything else"

GIVEN a IS A BOOLEAN
      b IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE f b TRUE  IS 1
DECIDE f a FALSE IS 2

-- A definition named like the binding of the later clauses, read by the
-- first clause.
`the result of clauses 2 to 3` MEANS 7

GIVEN c IS A Colour
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE u Red   IS `the result of clauses 2 to 3`
DECIDE u Green IS 2
DECIDE u Blue  IS 3

-- A definition named like an input of a group with no GIVEN.
`input 1` MEANS 9

DECIDE h Red   IS `input 1`
DECIDE h Green IS 2
DECIDE h Blue  IS 3

#EVAL paint (Circle Red)  Green -- "anything else"
#EVAL paint (Circle Blue) Red   -- "circle, paint red"
#EVAL f TRUE  FALSE             -- 2
#EVAL f FALSE TRUE              -- 1
#EVAL u Red                     -- 7
#EVAL u Green                   -- 2
#EVAL h Red                     -- 9
